[mysqld]
# Accept connections from any IP (required for Docker networking)
bind-address = 0.0.0.0

# Port to listen on
port = 3306

# User that runs the mysqld process
user = mysql

# Data directory (where database files are stored)
datadir = /var/lib/mysql

# Socket for local connections
socket = /run/mysqld/mysqld.sock

# Skip DNS resolution for faster connections
skip-name-resolve
```

---

### Line-by-Line Explanation

**`[mysqld]`**
Section header. Settings below this apply to the MySQL/MariaDB server daemon.

**`bind-address = 0.0.0.0`**
Listen on all network interfaces. The default `127.0.0.1` would only accept local connections. We need `0.0.0.0` so other containers can connect.

**`port = 3306`**
Standard MySQL/MariaDB port. WordPress will connect to `mariadb:3306`.

**`user = mysql`**
Run the server as the `mysql` user, not root. Security best practice.

**`datadir = /var/lib/mysql`**
Where database files are stored. This is where our volume mounts.

**`socket = /run/mysqld/mysqld.sock`**
Unix socket for local connections. Some tools use this instead of TCP.

**`skip-name-resolve`**
Don't try to resolve IP addresses to hostnames. Speeds up connections and avoids DNS issues inside Docker.

---

## Your Task

Create the file `srcs/requirements/mariadb/conf/50-server.cnf` with the content above.

